/** The negative findings, as data, so a section could count them. */
function negativeRows(M) {
  const mm = M.modes[M.main_mode];
  const eq = M.equivalence;
  const P = M.page;
  return [
    {
      cells: [
        'ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 must send less, since they keep less',
        P.zero12_send_same_as_dp
          ? `They send <b>exactly the same</b>: ${int(mm['1'].communication.per_step_sent.total)} bytes
             a step, the same as data parallelism. The saving is memory alone, at no extra
             communication — though for a moment each step ZeRO-2 holds a unit's full gradient, a peak of
             ${int(mm['2'].memory.peak_total)} bytes against ${int(mm['2'].memory.measured.total)}.`
          : `They do not send the same as data parallelism in this run.`,
      ],
    },
    {
      cells: [
        'ZeRO-3 removes the memory problem',
        `At the price of ${bpw(P.zero3_sent_over_dp)} times the communication, and each unit's weights
         gathered twice a step: its peak is ${int(mm['3'].memory.peak_total)} bytes against a steady
         ${int(mm['3'].memory.measured.total)}. And activations, often the larger bill, are not split
         by any stage.`,
      ],
      __mark: 'warn',
    },
    {
      cells: [
        'A cluster and one device doing the same arithmetic agree everywhere',
        P.key_bias_drifted_further
          ? `Everywhere but ${int(eq.key_bias_elements)} weights: ${sci(eq.fp32_weights_max_abs_except_key_bias)}
             at most elsewhere, ${sci(eq.fp32_weights_max_abs_key_bias)} on the attention key bias —
             ${bpw(P.key_bias_ratio)} times as far, in one run with one seed, a comparison of two
             maxima with no noise floor measured. The key bias adds the same number to every score in
             a row, and softmax ignores that, so its true gradient is exactly zero; what is computed is
             rounding noise (${sci(eq.key_bias_grad_step1_max_abs)}). AdamW divides each gradient by
             its own running size, which turns noise into a full step — and a different order of
             addition is different noise.`
          : `To ${sci(eq.fp32_weights_max_abs_except_key_bias)} everywhere, and in this run the
             ${int(eq.key_bias_elements)} key-bias weights did not drift further than the rest.`,
      ],
      __mark: 'warn',
    },
    {
      cells: [
        'bf16 training reproduces fp32',
        `Closely, not exactly: the two runs' total weight updates point the same way to a cosine of
         ${eq.mixed_update_cosine.toFixed(6)}, and the losses differ by ${sci(eq.mixed_loss_max_rel)}
         relative. Close is not identical, and the page does not call it identical.`,
      ],
    },
    {
      cells: [
        'An update that reads one element to write one element cannot depend on how a buffer is cut',
        `On this CPU it can. PyTorch's fused optimiser kernels give different bits depending on where
         a buffer is cut — consistent with a vectorised loop and its scalar tail rounding differently,
         and a shard boundary moving elements between them. The optimiser here is built from
         operations that each round once, and a test cuts buffers at deliberately awkward places.`,
      ],
      __mark: 'warn',
    },
    {
      cells: [
        'The ledger counts what a device holds',
        `Only if it charges the memory behind each tensor rather than the tensor's own size. A slice
         cut from a full buffer keeps the whole buffer alive, so the ledger charges each storage once,
         in full; charging the slice would report a fraction of what is actually held.`,
      ],
      __mark: 'warn',
    },
  ];
}

/* ============================================================== 9 · conclusion */

function chapterConclusion(M) {
  const P = M.page;
  const mm = M.modes[M.main_mode];
  const L = P.slider;
  const dpn = M.topology.devices_per_node;
  const ladder0 = M.ladder.rows.filter((r) => r.stage === 0).sort((a, b) => a.world_size - b.world_size);
  const inside = ladder0.find((r) => r.world_size === dpn);
  const across = ladder0.find((r) => r.world_size > dpn);
  const s = section(
    'conclusion',
    'conclusion',
    'The verdict',
    'ZeRO moves bytes; it does not remove them',
    [
      `Every stage pays the same ${spell(P.per_weight[M.main_mode]['0'].total)} bytes per weight in
       total, and computes the same thing. What a stage decides is how much of that bill each device
       carries — and, through the floor, whether adding devices helps at all.`,
      `${
        P.zero12_send_same_as_dp
          ? `ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 cost no extra communication: they keep a half-way result data
             parallelism throws away.`
          : `ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 do not match data parallelism's communication in this run.`
      } ZeRO-3 is the only stage without a floor, and the only one that costs more traffic. And the
       steady figures are not the whole bill: for a moment each step ZeRO-2 reaches ${int(mm['2'].memory.peak_total)}
       bytes against a steady ${int(mm['2'].memory.measured.total)}, ZeRO-3
       ${int(mm['3'].memory.peak_total)} against ${int(mm['3'].memory.measured.total)} — and
       activations, which no stage splits, come on top of all of it.`,
      inside && across && !absent(inside.comm_seconds_per_step) && !absent(across.comm_seconds_per_step)
        ? `And the trade is not only bytes. On the assumed links, once a ring leaves one machine of
           ${int(dpn)}, its slowest link sets the pace: data parallelism's communication at
           ${M.ladder.params / 1e9} billion weights would go from ${inside.comm_seconds_per_step.toFixed(2)} s
           a step at N&nbsp;=&nbsp;${inside.world_size} to ${across.comm_seconds_per_step.toFixed(2)} s at
           N&nbsp;=&nbsp;${across.world_size}. Those are assumed bandwidths, not a measurement; the size
           of the jump is set entirely by the assumed ratio between the two kinds of link.`
        : '',
    ].filter(Boolean),
    { short: 'The verdict', sub: 'where the bytes live' }
  );

  const box = el('div', 'takeaway');
  box.innerHTML = `<b>The check to carry away.</b> Before adding devices to fix a memory problem, add
    up what each device keeps <i>whole</i>. If that alone is more than the card, no number of devices
    will help${
      L.never_fits['1']
        ? ` — that is ZeRO-1's ${gib(L.floor_gib['1'])} GiB at ${M.ladder.params / 1e9} billion weights`
        : ''
    } — and only a stage that splits more of the bill will.`;
  s.append(box);
}

